         0,
         "The correct approach computes: cosine similarity of unit vectors is their dot product. A·B = 0.8, A·C = 0. B is clearly most similar. 'Both are unit vectors' is irrelevant — cosine already normalizes; direction is what matters, and B points mostly along A while C is orthogonal.",
         "For unit vectors, cosine similarity is just the dot product. Compute both.",
         ["Compute cosine similarity", "Interpret similarity scores", "Reason geometrically"], 10,
